  "tldr": "Synthetic Blips identifies dynamic path values under latent time-varying confounding but leaves schedule comparison without a joint sampling law. For a fixed target unit, finite horizon, fixed rank, one-lag LTV model, and finite budget-feasible path menu, this core constructs a covariate-row-cross-fitted orthogonal recursive SBE-PCR estimator. Its focal theorem is a uniform joint root-\\(N\\) expansion obtained by differentiating the entire backward graph. A leave-one-unit-out replay estimator recovers the full covariance, including off-diagonal terms from donors reused across nodes and paths. The resulting Gaussian law yields simultaneous path bands, an honest set containing every maximizing path under ties, and an explicit correlated finite-Gaussian local-gap regret limit.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "The 2025 Synthetic-Blips paper stops at pointwise consistency. Its backward estimator repeatedly reuses fitted donor outcomes, so neither scalar Synthetic-Interventions limits nor static synthetic-control prediction intervals deliver the joint influence law required for schedule choice.",
    "niche": "Fixed-horizon panels with many noisy proxy rows create an unusual asymptotic regime: cross-sectional donor noise is first order, spectral and row-regression errors must be orthogonalized, and the policy object is a correlated finite vector of path values.",
    "fill": "The project propagates exact source coefficients through the SBE graph, proves all cross-fitted spectral remainders negligible, estimates covariance by residual replay, and carries the joint limit through max and argmax operations relevant to the Korean export-support application."
  },
  "related_work": "Agarwal, Han, Saha, Syrgkanis, and Yoon (2025) provide the writing and model anchor: their LTV identification theorem expresses every path mean by baseline and backward-recursive blips, and their consistency proof repeatedly imports PCR projected-error bounds without an influence representation. Agarwal, Shah, and Shen (2024) provide the nearest PCR limit theorem, but its Gaussian term is averaged over a growing post-period for one intervention mean rather than over cross-sectional donors in a finite backward graph. Chernozhukov et al. (2018) supply the orthogonality and cross-fitting template, while Chernozhukov, Hansen, Liao, and Zhu (2023) supply the separated-eigenspace rotation and refitting template; both are method exemplars rather than theorem-level targets. Ben-Michael, Feller, and Rothstein (2021) motivate the row-space augmentation, but their correction is static. Cattaneo, Feng, and Titiunik (2021) distinguish weight uncertainty from prediction uncertainty for static synthetic controls. Chernozhukov, Chetverikov, and Kato (2017) provide the Gaussian approximation after the recursive influence array has been derived. Andrews, Kitagawa, and McCloskey (2024) study inference for a selected coordinate, whereas the present inferential object is a set containing all population-maximizing paths. Hirano and Porter (2009) motivate root-\\(N\\) local treatment-choice analysis, and Chernozhukov, Lee, Rosen, and Sun (2025) motivate Gaussian confidence-aware policy choice once a valid joint covariance is available.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The condition \\(p_N/N^{2+\\delta}\\to\\infty\\) is a sufficient early-kill regime, not a claimed necessity result. The open frontier question keeps the sharp proxy-growth requirement visible. Weak factors, rank overestimation, a growing horizon, and a growing path menu are excluded from this first version.",
  "honest_scope": "The exact oracle coefficient propagation and the two-period covariance witness are algebraically plausible starting points. The load-bearing unresolved step is uniform root-\\(N\\) equivalence of the feasible orthogonal recursion and its oracle version. Conditional on that step, the finite-dimensional Gaussian, replay covariance, simultaneous-band, maximizing-set, and local-regret arguments are standard transformations but still require careful uniformity under ties. When \\(T=2\\) and the graph has no reused donors, the construction reduces to the scalar Synthetic-Interventions decomposition; when the legal shared-donor witness is used, it instead yields covariance \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&3\\end{pmatrix}\\).",
